      "difficulty": "knowledge-check",
      "stem": "To find an angle when you know the opposite and adjacent sides, you use:",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "a",
          "text": "$\\theta = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}\\right)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "b",
          "text": "$\\theta = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}\\right)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "text": "$\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}\\right)$"
        },
        {
          "id": "d",
          "text": "$\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\text{adj}}{\\text{opp}}\\right)$"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "c",
      "explanation": "When you have the opposite and adjacent sides, use $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}$, so $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\text{opp}}{\\text{adj}}\\right)$."
